{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 15,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.047,
            0.418,
            0.065
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.091,
            0.795,
            0.116
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.5 Right–Triangles Having Integer Sidelengths"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.124,
            0.846,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": "In his Śulba-Sūtra (Verse 1.13) Baudhāyana lists a number of integer triples $( a , b , c )$ that form the sidelengths of a right-triangle and therefore satisfy $a ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 2 } = c ^ { 2 }$ . These include (3, 4, 5), (5, 12, 13) (8, 15, 17), (7, 24, 25), (12, 35, 37), and (15, 36, 39)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.198,
            0.847,
            0.274
          ],
          "content": "For this reason, such triples $( a ,   b ,   c )$ that form the sidelengths of a right triangle (equivalently, satisfy $a ^ { 2 }   +   b ^ { 2 }   =   c ^ { 2 } )$ **a**re called Baudhāyana **triples.** They are also called **Baudhāyana-Pythagoras triples, Pythagorean triples,** and **right-angled triangle triples.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.282,
            0.801,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": "？ List down all the Baudhāyana triples with numbers less than or equal to 20."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.267,
            0.904,
            0.319
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.337,
            0.683,
            0.359
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Is there an unending sequence of Baudhāyana triples?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.362,
            0.847,
            0.416
          ],
          "content": "Mathematicians have answered this question and have found a method to generate all such triples! Let us take a few steps in this direction."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.416,
            0.659,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": "We have seen that (3, 4, 5) is a Baudhāyana triple."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.445,
            0.497,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": "Is (30, 40, 50) a Baudhāyana triple?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.478,
            0.533,
            0.498
          ],
          "content": "Is (300, 400, 500) a Baudhāyana triple?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.501,
            0.845,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": "The list of Baudhāyana triples having numbers less than or equal to 20 contains the following triples"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.306,
            0.543,
            0.691,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": "(3, 4, 5), (6, 8, 10), (9, 12, 15), (12, 16, 20)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.578,
            0.518,
            0.602
          ],
          "content": "Do you see any pattern among them?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.604,
            0.846,
            0.643
          ],
          "content": "All these triples can be obtained by multiplying each term of (3, 4, 5) by a certain positive integer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.652,
            0.845,
            0.692
          ],
          "content": "? Can we form a conjecture on Baudhāyana triples based on this observation?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.702,
            0.845,
            0.742
          ],
          "content": "**Conjecture:** (3k, 4k, 5k) is a Baudhāyana triple, where k is any positive integer."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.754,
            0.275,
            0.778
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Is this true?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.78,
            0.528,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": "We have to check if $( 3 k ) ^ { 2 } + ( 4 k ) ^ { 2 } = ( 5 k ) ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.804,
            0.752,
            0.824
          ],
          "content": "We have $( 3 k ) ^ { 2 } + ( 4 k ) ^ { 2 } = 9 k ^ { 2 } + 1 6 k ^ { 2 } = 2 5 k ^ { 2 } ,$ , which is equal to (5k)<sup>2</sup>."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.828,
            0.593,
            0.849
          ],
          "content": "So, (3k, 4k, 5k) is indeed a Baudhāyana triple."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.851,
            0.753,
            0.873
          ],
          "content": "This shows that there are infinitely many Baudhāyana triples."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.876,
            0.564,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": "Can we further generalise the conjecture?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.093,
            0.922,
            0.129,
            0.942
          ],
          "content": "48"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
